  "articleBody": "When your kitchen feels dated, deciding whether to replace or reface your cabinets comes down to layout, box durability, and your long-term goals.\n\nRefacing involves keeping your existing cabinet boxes while replacing the doors, drawer fronts, and hardware, along with applying matching veneer to the exterior frames. It works well when your current layout functions smoothly, your cabinet boxes are structurally solid, and you simply want a cosmetic refresh. Refacing typically takes less time and costs less upfront.\n\nFull replacement means removing all existing cabinet boxes down to the drywall and installing brand-new cabinetry from the ground up. This option is necessary if your cabinet boxes have water damage, sagging shelves, or broken joints, or if you want to alter your floor plan to gain better storage, improve workflow, or accommodate larger appliances.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options:\nEvaluate total project scope. Refacing cannot fix poor cabinet layout, broken particleboard, or plumbing clearance problems. Replacement allows you to reconfigure your workspace, inspect the walls and subfloors behind the cabinets, and update rough plumbing or electrical lines safely.\n\nIf your kitchen needs a fresh layout or structural upgrades, Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC provides complete kitchen renovation support across the Denver metro area. Call 303-505-0866 to discuss your options.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
